BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:IU Arts & Humanities Institute
DTSTART:20260922T210000Z
DTEND:20260922T220000Z
LOCATION:Maxwell Hall
SUMMARY:Meet the Author: Matt Cook on Writing Queer History
DESCRIPTION:Matt Cook is the inaugural Jonathan Cooper Professor of the H
 istory of Sexuality at the University of Oxford\, and Co-Director (with P
 rof. Sarah Knott) of the Centre for Women's\, Gender and Queer Histories.
  He specialises in modern and contemporary queer\, British\, urban and pu
 blic history\, and has published widely\, with books including London and
  the Culture of Homosexuality (2003)\, Queer Domesticities (2014)\, Queer
  Beyond London (2022) and Writing Queer History (2026). He is a Fellow of
  the Royal Historical Society\, an editor of History Workshop Journal\, a
 nd has advised on a wide range of community\, museum\, archive and herita
 ge sector projects\, nationally and internationally. Before joining Oxfor
 d's History Faculty in 2023\, he was Professor of Modern History at Birkb
 eck\, University of London\, where he co-directed the Raphael Samuel Hist
 ory Centre.  This event is co-sponsored by the College Arts + Humanities 
 Institute\, the Kinsey Institute\, and the University of Oxford's Centre 
 for Women's\, Gender and Queer Histories. For more information\, or if yo
 u have an accommodation request\, please email cahi@iu.edu or call 812-85
 6-1169.
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p><strong>Matt Cook</strong> is the inaugur
 al Jonathan Cooper Professor of the History of Sexuality at the Universit
 y of Oxford\, and Co-Director (with Prof. Sarah Knott) of the Centre for 
 Women's\, Gender and Queer Histories. He specialises in modern and contem
 porary queer\, British\, urban and public history\, and has published wid
 ely\, with books including <em>London and the Culture of Homosexuality</e
 m> (2003)\, <em>Queer Domesticities</em> (2014)\, <em>Queer Beyond London
 </em> (2022) and <em>Writing Queer History</em> (2026). He is a Fellow of
  the Royal Historical Society\, an editor of History Workshop Journal\, a
 nd has advised on a wide range of community\, museum\, archive and herita
 ge sector projects\, nationally and internationally. Before joining Oxfor
 d's History Faculty in 2023\, he was Professor of Modern History at Birkb
 eck\, University of London\, where he co-directed the Raphael Samuel Hist
 ory Centre.</p><p>  This event is co-sponsored by the College Arts + Huma
 nities Institute\, the Kinsey Institute\, and the University of Oxford's 
 Centre for Women's\, Gender and Queer Histories. For more information\, o
 r if you have an accommodation request\, please email cahi@iu.edu or call
  812-856-1169.</p>
