BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:IU Arts & Humanities Institute
DTSTART:20260923T150000Z
DTEND:20260923T160000Z
LOCATION:Kinsey Institute
SUMMARY:The Bad Habits of Albert Quint: Queer Labor and Everyday Lives wi
 th Author Matthew Cook
DESCRIPTION:Kinsey Institute Guest Lecture  &#160\;Wednesday\, September 
 2311:00 AM - 12:00 PM  In-Person\, Lindley Hall 328 (Register below)  Onl
 ine via Zoom (Register here (https://iu.zoom.us/webinar/register/WN_ZxYcE
 zJhQWWy2eOWSd-5HQ))  &#160\;  Every night for the first three months of 1
 937\, 23-year-old Albert Quint completed a page of his diary in the house
  he shared with his working-class parents in rural Wiltshire (England).  
 He described how his shopwork sustained his obsession with cinema and fun
 ded the acting classes which he saw as a stepping stone to a career on th
 e stage. Alongside\, he alluded to his 'bad habits' and his erotic intere
 st in the soldiers stationed at nearby military bases.  Albert's testimon
 y is the cue for this talk\, which explores how work – most especially 
 in the service sector\, theatre\, and armed forces - was practically\, as
 pirationally\, and erotically significant to queer everyday lives in inte
 r and post war Britain. Tracing Albert's life back and forward from his t
 antalising diary entries shines light on the literal labour involved in '
 doing being ordinary' (Sacks\, 1984) and\, I argue\, in 'doing being devi
 ant' (Love\, 2015).Matt Cook is the inaugural Jonathan Cooper Professor o
 f the History of Sexuality at the University of Oxford\, and Co-Director 
 (with Prof. Sarah Knott) of the Centre for Women's Gender and Queer Histo
 ry. He specialises in modern and contemporary queer\, British\, urban and
  public history\, and has published widely\, with books including London 
 and the Culture of Homosexuality (2003)\, Queer Domesticities (2014)\, Qu
 eer Beyond London (2022) and Writing Queer History (2026). He is a Fellow
  of the Royal Historical Society\, an editor of History Workshop Journal\
 , and has advised on a wide range of community\, museum\, archive and her
 itage sector projects\, nationally and internationally. Before joining Ox
 ford's History Faculty in 2023\, he was Professor of Modern History at Bi
 rkbeck\, University of London\, where he co-directed the Raphael Samuel H
 istory Centre.  &#160\;Recent publications:Matt Cook\, Writing Queer Hist
 ory (2026)Writing Queer History explores the remarkable and various ways 
 in which historians and others have contended with the queer past. From t
 he radical ferment of the 1960s and the emergence of lesbian and gay hist
 ory\, the book charts a course to the culture wars and queer history of o
 ur present day. How have LGBT and queer historians responded to their cul
 tural and political contexts\, to broader and shifting historical practic
 es\, and to queer and other theoretical perspectives? How have they navig
 ated sources\, fragments\, gaps and silences? And what has their work rev
 ealed and achieved?  In this state-of-field guide\, Matt Cook surveys res
 earch and writing from across the last 60 years by activist\, community a
 nd academic historians\, working within and beyond university settings\, 
 and across different disciplines and genres. He argues that queer history
  has been a connective\, intersectional and boundary-crossing practice. A
 t its best\, it has illuminated broad social\, cultural\, political and h
 istorical dynamics\, and multivalent ideas and experiences of desire\, id
 entity\, and community. Over five chapters\, each with an autobiographica
 l coda\, the book shows how the personal is not only political but profou
 ndly historical.Matt Cook\, Rebecca Jennings\, E-J Scott and Justin Bengr
 y\, eds\, A Queer Scrapbook: Britain and Ireland since 1945  A beautifull
 y illustrated compendium of LGBTIQ+ life\, A Queer Scrapbook offers a ric
 h archive of histories from across Britain and Ireland. Brimming with int
 erviews\, newspaper clippings\, photographs\, and flyers\, it traces urba
 n\, rural\, and regional queer experiences from 1945 to the present. Comm
 entaries and short essays introduce a changing queer landscape\, organize
 d around four themes: home and family\, socializing\, and sex\, arts and 
 culture\, and activism and community. The book explores domestic life and
  parenting\, reveals the unexpected places where LGBTIQ+ people gathered 
 for fun\, highlights the importance of creative expression\, and document
 s campaigns for justice and equal rights. Rooted in the tradition of coll
 ecting as a way for marginalised people to assert identity and community\
 , A Queer Scrapbook vividly captures the diversity of queer and trans liv
 es across the British and Irish isles since the Second World War.For onli
 ne Zoom registration\, please click here. (https://iu.zoom.us/webinar/reg
 ister/WN_ZxYcEzJhQWWy2eOWSd-5HQ)For in-person registration\, please RSVP 
 below.
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<h5 style="text-align: center\;"><strong>Kin
 sey Institute Guest Lecture</strong></h5><h5 style="text-align: center\;"
 >  &#160\;</h5><h5 style="text-align: center\;"><strong>Wednesday\, Septe
 mber 23</strong></h5><h5 style="text-align: center\;"><strong>11:00 AM - 
 12:00 PM</strong></h5><p style="text-align: center\;">  In-Person\, Lindl
 ey Hall 328 (Register below)<br />  Online via Zoom (<a href="https://iu.
 zoom.us/webinar/register/WN_ZxYcEzJhQWWy2eOWSd-5HQ" target="_blank" rel="
 noopener">Register here</a>)</p><p style="text-align: left\;">  &#160\;</
 p><p>  Every night for the first three months of 1937\, 23-year-old Alber
 t Quint completed a page of his diary in the house he shared with his wor
 king-class parents in rural Wiltshire (England).</p><p>  He described how
  his shopwork sustained his obsession with cinema and funded the acting c
 lasses which he saw as a stepping stone to a career on the stage. Alongsi
 de\, he alluded to his 'bad habits' and his erotic interest in the soldie
 rs stationed at nearby military bases.</p><p>  Albert's testimony is the 
 cue for this talk\, which explores how work – most especially in the se
 rvice sector\, theatre\, and armed forces - was practically\, aspirationa
 lly\, and erotically significant to queer everyday lives in inter and pos
 t war Britain. Tracing Albert's life back and forward from his tantalisin
 g diary entries shines light on the literal labour involved in 'doing bei
 ng ordinary' (Sacks\, 1984) and\, I argue\, in 'doing being deviant' (Lov
 e\, 2015).</p><p><strong>Matt Cook</strong> is the inaugural Jonathan Coo
 per Professor of the History of Sexuality at the University of Oxford\, a
 nd Co-Director (with Prof. Sarah Knott) of the Centre for Women's Gender 
 and Queer History. He specialises in modern and contemporary queer\, Brit
 ish\, urban and public history\, and has published widely\, with books in
 cluding <em>London and the Culture of Homosexuality</em> (2003)\, <em>Que
