SUMMARY:Venezuelan Politics in the Wake of Crisis
DESCRIPTION:Against the backdrop of political upheaval\, foreign interven
 tion\, natural disaster\, and long-standing infrastructure challenges\, V
 enezuelan Politics in the Wake of Crisis will bring together scholars and
  a human rights advocate for an interdisciplinary examination of contempo
 rary Venezuela.  The symposium will feature Verónica Zubillaga of the Un
 iversity of Illinois Chicago and Yoletty Bracho of Avignon University\, j
 oining in person\; Magdalena López of the University of Notre Dame and t
 he University Institute of Lisbon\, joining virtually\; and Venezuelan at
 torney and human rights advocate Venus Marina Faddoul Barrios\, director 
 of the NGO 100% Estrógeno\, joining virtually from Spain.  Drawing on ex
 pertise in political participation\, urban and state violence\, cultural 
 criticism\, human rights\, and gender justice\, the panelists will examin
 e how Venezuela's continuing crises have affected political life\, civil 
 society\, migration and displacement\, and regional relations. The progra
 m will also include a screening of 22 de mayo (1969) by Cine Urgente.  Or
 ganized by the Center for Research on Race and Ethnicity in Society (CRRE
 S)\, in collaboration with the Center for Latin American and Caribbean St
 udies (CLACS)\, and the Hamilton Lugar School of Global and International
  Studies (HLS).
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  Against the backdrop of political uphea
 val\, foreign intervention\, natural disaster\, and long-standing infrast
 ructure challenges\, <em><strong>Venezuelan Politics in the Wake of Crisi
 s</strong></em> will bring together scholars and a human rights advocate 
 for an interdisciplinary examination of contemporary Venezuela.</p><p>  T
 he symposium will feature <strong>Verónica Zubillaga</strong> of the Uni
 versity of Illinois Chicago and <strong>Yoletty Bracho</strong> of Avigno
 n University\, joining in person\; <strong>Magdalena López</strong> of t
 he University of Notre Dame and the University Institute of Lisbon\, join
 ing virtually\; and Venezuelan attorney and human rights advocate <strong
 >Venus Marina Faddoul Barrios</strong>\, director of the NGO 100% Estróg
 eno\, joining virtually from Spain.</p><p>  Drawing on expertise in polit
 ical participation\, urban and state violence\, cultural criticism\, huma
 n rights\, and gender justice\, the panelists will examine how Venezuela'
 s continuing crises have affected political life\, civil society\, migrat
 ion and displacement\, and regional relations. The program will also incl
 ude a screening of <em><strong>22 de mayo</strong></em> (1969) by <strong
 >Cine Urgente</strong>.</p><p>  Organized by the Center for Research on R
 ace and Ethnicity in Society (CRRES)\, in collaboration with the Center f
