SUMMARY:Taiwan Studies Initiative: A Chip Odyssey Film Screening and Disc
 ussion
DESCRIPTION:Special Documentary Screening • Faculty Panel • Reception
 October 3 | 5:00–8:00 PMShreve Auditorium  Global and International Stu
 dies Building  355 N. Eagleson Ave.\, BloomingtonHow did Taiwan build a s
 emiconductor industry from scratch—and transform it into a global techn
 ological force?  Directed by Chu-chen Hsiao\, A Chip Odyssey tells the st
 ory of Taiwan's semiconductor industry through interviews with more than 
 80 people who witnessed and shaped its development—from pioneering engi
 neers and female factory workers to policymakers\, technology veterans\, 
 and a new generation of engineers confronting new challenges.  Join us fo
 r a special screening followed by a multidisciplinary conversation with I
 ndiana University faculty exploring Taiwan's semiconductor industry\, tec
 hnological innovation\, and its broader global significance.PROGRAM5:00 P
 M  Welcome Remarks5:10 PMA Chip Odyssey Screening6:45 PM  Faculty Panel D
 iscussion featuring:&#160\;Chen-Ting Liao\, Associate Professor\, Departm
 ent of PhysicsIsak Nti Asare\, Co-Director\, Cybersecurity and Global Pol
 icy Program\; Assistant Dean for Undergraduate Education and Student Affa
 irs&#160\;Sagar Samtani\, Associate Professor\, Director of Kelley's Data
  Science and Artificial Intelligence LabChung-chieh Shan\, Associate Prof
 essor\, Luddy School of Informatics\, Computing\, and Engineering7:15–8
 :00 PM  Light ReceptionJOIN THE CONVERSATION  Discover the people and his
 tory behind Taiwan's semiconductor transformation and engage with IU spec
 ialists from across disciplines for a deeper conversation about technolog
 y\, innovation\, and Taiwan's IT sector.Organized by  IU Taiwan Studies I
 nitiative  East Asian Studies Center  Taipei Economic and Cultural Office
  (TECO) in Chicago
