BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:IU Arts & Humanities Institute
DTSTART:20261005T160000Z
DTEND:20261005T173000Z
LOCATION:GLOBAL &amp\; INTERNATIONAL STUDIES BUILDING
GEO:39.170002;-86.517492
SUMMARY:EASC Colloquium: The Courtesan's Memory\, Voice\, and Late Ming D
 rama - Peng Xu
DESCRIPTION:Peng Xu's The Courtesan's Memory\, Voice\, and Late Ming Dram
 a argues that courtesans of the era played an active role in the theater 
 and their impact manifested in Chinese literary history\, albeit conceale
 d or erased. In the late Ming dynasty (1368–1644)\, theater was dominat
 ed by male performers with professional actresses largely absent from the
  stage\, unlike in the Yuan dynasty (1271–1368)\, where women performed
  without restriction in theater troupes. Despite the prevailing assumptio
 n that women played no part in the theatrical movement\, The Courtesan's 
 Memory\, Voice\, and Late Ming Drama illuminates how courtesans\, as seri
 ous theater artists and playwrights\, left a significant mark on the male
 -dominated dramatic and literary landscapes. This study profoundly remaps
  the textbook narratives of gender bias\, challenges modern prejudices pr
 evalent in the literary history of Chinese drama\, and reveals how theatr
 ical conditions and writing styles shifted to accommodate courtesan perfo
 rmances.  Utilizing a wide range of sources such as scripts\, city guides
 \, biographies\, diaries\, letters\, wood-block illustrations\, and paint
 ings\, this study distinguishes itself from earlier studies of Chinese co
 urtesans by not thematizing courtesans for their allegorical values\, but
  rather pinpointing their agency in shaping the features of Chinese theat
 er and recognizing courtesans as a source of creativity and development i
 n the medium. Xu's focus on courtesan performance culture allows for fres
 h readings of canonical Chinese plays\, opening up new ways of understand
 ing the history of Chinese drama written by women\, thus deviating from s
 tandard historiography based solely on the writings of men.About the auth
 or: Peng Xu received her Ph.D. from the University of Chicago and was a p
 ostdoctoral fellow at UC Berkeley. Currently Associate Professor at Shang
 haiTech University\, she has held academic appointments at institutions a
 cross the U.S. and Asia. She actively promote cross-disciplinary and tran
 snational dialogues in Chinese literary and performance studies\, with a 
 particular emphasis on the agency of actresses and women poets. She has p
 ublished in leading journals including Harvard Journal of Asiatic Studies
 \, Late Imperial China\, T'oung Pao\, CLEAR: Chinese Literature\, Essays\
 , and Reviews\, and Ars Orientalis. Her research-related activities have 
 been funded by the Kyujanggak Institute at Seoul National University (202
 1)\, Fulbright American Scholar program (2020)\, ACLS/Henry-Luce Foundati
 on (2018)\, among others.  An award-winning scholar-performer specializin
 g in Kunqu and Peking Opera\, Dr. Xu integrates scholarship and stage pra
 ctice\, bringing historical insight and aesthetic precision into every pe
 rformance.
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p><strong>Peng Xu</strong>'s <em>The Courte
 san's Memory\, Voice\, and Late Ming Drama</em> argues that courtesans of
  the era played an active role in the theater and their impact manifested
  in Chinese literary history\, albeit concealed or erased. In the late Mi
 ng dynasty (1368–1644)\, theater was dominated by male performers with 
 professional actresses largely absent from the stage\, unlike in the Yuan
  dynasty (1271–1368)\, where women performed without restriction in the
 ater troupes. Despite the prevailing assumption that women played no part
  in the theatrical movement\, <em>The Courtesan's Memory\, Voice\, and La
 te Ming Drama</em> illuminates how courtesans\, as serious theater artist
 s and playwrights\, left a significant mark on the male-dominated dramati
 c and literary landscapes. This study profoundly remaps the textbook narr
 atives of gender bias\, challenges modern prejudices prevalent in the lit
 erary history of Chinese drama\, and reveals how theatrical conditions an
 d writing styles shifted to accommodate courtesan performances.</p><p>  U
 tilizing a wide range of sources such as scripts\, city guides\, biograph
 ies\, diaries\, letters\, wood-block illustrations\, and paintings\, this
  study distinguishes itself from earlier studies of Chinese courtesans by
  not thematizing courtesans for their allegorical values\, but rather pin
 pointing their agency in shaping the features of Chinese theater and reco
 gnizing courtesans as a source of creativity and development in the mediu
 m. Xu's focus on courtesan performance culture allows for fresh readings 
 of canonical Chinese plays\, opening up new ways of understanding the his
 tory of Chinese drama written by women\, thus deviating from standard his
 toriography based solely on the writings of men.</p><p><strong>About the 
 author:</strong> Peng Xu received her Ph.D. from the University of Chicag
 o and was a postdoctoral fellow at UC Berkeley. Currently Associate Profe
 ssor at ShanghaiTech University\, she has held academic appointments at i
 nstitutions across the U.S. and Asia. She actively promote cross-discipli
 nary and transnational dialogues in Chinese literary and performance stud
 ies\, with a particular emphasis on the agency of actresses and women poe
 ts. She has published in leading journals including Harvard Journal of As
 iatic Studies\, Late Imperial China\, T'oung Pao\, CLEAR: Chinese Literat
 ure\, Essays\, and Reviews\, and Ars Orientalis. Her research-related act
 ivities have been funded by the Kyujanggak Institute at Seoul National Un
 iversity (2021)\, Fulbright American Scholar program (2020)\, ACLS/Henry-
 Luce Foundation (2018)\, among others.</p><p>  An award-winning scholar-p
 erformer specializing in Kunqu and Peking Opera\, Dr. Xu integrates schol
 arship and stage practice\, bringing historical insight and aesthetic pre
 cision into every performance.</p>
UID:20261005T160000Z-2370431@events.iu.edu
