BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:IU Arts & Humanities Institute
DTSTART:20261006T153000Z
DTEND:20261006T170000Z
LOCATION:Maxwell Hall
SUMMARY:Lunch and Moderated Q&A with Carol Anderson
DESCRIPTION:Carol Anderson will join Randal Jelks (https://aaads.indiana.
 edu/about/faculty/jelks-randal.html) (Ruth N. Halls Professor of African 
 American and African Diaspora Studies) for a conversation about her resea
 rch and career as a public writer\, reflecting on the importance of inter
 secting the arts and humanities with civic engagement. The discussion wil
 l be followed by an audience Q&amp\;A\, and this event will be limited to
  the first 60 people to register.  Professor Anderson will also give a pu
 blic lecture\, "One Person\, No Vote: How Voter Suppression is Destroying
  Our Democracy\," at the Buskirk-Chumley Theatre on Tuesday\, October 6 a
 t 7pm. The event is free\, but ticketed. Tickets are available at the BCT
 's website (https://buskirkchumley.org/events/one-person-no-vote-a-lectur
 e-and-q-a-with-carol-anderson-october-6-2026/) and box office.  Carol And
 erson is the Robert W. Woodruff Professor of African American Studies at 
 Emory University. She is the author of several bestselling books includin
 g: The Second: Race and Guns in a Fatally Unequal America (2020)\; One Pe
 rson\, No Vote: How Voter Suppression is Destroying Our Democracy (2018)\
 , which was long-listed for the National Book Award in Non-Fiction\; and 
 the critically-acclaimed White Rage: The Unspoken Truth of Our Racial Div
 ide (2016)\, which won the 2016 National Book Critics Circle Award for Cr
 iticism\, was a New York Times Editor's Pick\, and was listed on the Zora
  List of 100 Best Books by Black Women Authors since 1850.  Professor And
 erson is a member of the American Academy of Arts and Sciences and the Am
 erican Philosophical Society\, and a recipient of the Ella Baker Lifetime
  Achievement Award from the Hurston/Wright Foundation. Her groundbreaking
  research has been supported by fellowships from the American Council of 
 Learned Societies\, the Ford Foundation\, National Humanities Center\, Ha
 rvard University\, and the Gilder Lehrman Institute of American History. 
  This event is presented with generous support from the College of Arts &
 amp\; Sciences and the Ruth N. Halls Humanities Fund.
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  Carol Anderson will join <a href="https
 ://aaads.indiana.edu/about/faculty/jelks-randal.html">Randal Jelks</a> (R
 uth N. Halls Professor of African American and African Diaspora Studies) 
 for a conversation about her research and career as a public writer\, ref
 lecting on the importance of intersecting the arts and humanities with ci
 vic engagement. The discussion will be followed by an audience Q&amp\;A\,
  and this event will be limited to the first 60 people to register.</p><p
 >  Professor Anderson will also give a public lecture\, "One Person\, No 
 Vote: How Voter Suppression is Destroying Our Democracy\," at the Buskirk
 -Chumley Theatre on Tuesday\, October 6 at 7pm. The event is free\, but t
 icketed. Tickets are available at the BCT's <a href="https://buskirkchuml
 ey.org/events/one-person-no-vote-a-lecture-and-q-a-with-carol-anderson-oc
 tober-6-2026/">website</a> and box office.</p><p>  Carol Anderson is the 
 Robert W. Woodruff Professor of African American Studies at Emory Univers
 ity. She is the author of several bestselling books including: <em>The Se
 cond: Race and Guns in a Fatally Unequal America</em> (2020)\; <em>One Pe
 rson\, No Vote: How Voter Suppression is Destroying Our Democracy</em> (2
 018)\, which was long-listed for the National Book Award in Non-Fiction\;
  and the critically-acclaimed <em>White Rage: The Unspoken Truth of Our R
 acial Divide</em> (2016)\, which won the 2016 National Book Critics Circl
 e Award for Criticism\, was a <em>New York Times</em> Editor's Pick\, and
  was listed on the Zora List of 100 Best Books by Black Women Authors sin
 ce 1850.</p><p>  Professor Anderson is a member of the American Academy o
 f Arts and Sciences and the American Philosophical Society\, and a recipi
 ent of the Ella Baker Lifetime Achievement Award from the Hurston/Wright 
 Foundation. Her groundbreaking research has been supported by fellowships
  from the American Council of Learned Societies\, the Ford Foundation\, N
 ational Humanities Center\, Harvard University\, and the Gilder Lehrman I
 nstitute of American History.</p><p>  This event is presented with genero
 us support from the College of Arts &amp\; Sciences and the Ruth N. Halls
  Humanities Fund.</p>
