GEO:39.170002;-86.517492
SUMMARY:Tobias Center Research Seminar: The Grid's Rural Edge
DESCRIPTION:This installment of the Tobias Center research seminar series
 \, entitled "The grid's rural edge: Rethinking energy- and land-based cli
 mate finance in upland Laos\," features Mike Dwyer\, Associate Professor 
 in IU's Department of Geography.Abstract:Climate finance is often separat
 ed conceptually and operationally along lines that divide energy systems 
 from land use. At a moment of urgency in both societal efforts to address
  climate change and specialist debates about climate finance\, this paper
  problematizes the distinction between energy- and land-based climate fin
 ance by examining how rural electricity access influences land and forest
  dynamics in the global South. Drawing on research in Laos\, the paper th
 eorizes what I call the land-energy nexus by examining the conditions und
 er which rural electrification can help improve farmer-controlled value a
 dding\, and thus improve the performance of transnational efforts to redu
 ce deforestation and forest degradation (REDD+). In doing so\, it seeks t
 o critically extend the concept of "land sparing"\, the theory of change 
 that underlies many REDD+ programs but has proven elusive in practice. Th
 e paper proposes two intersecting scales for understanding the land-energ
 y nexus in the global South: first\, a system- or large-landscape scale w
 here land-tenure security\, labor mobility and electricity financing each
  bear on forest-change dynamics\; and second\, a more localized landscape
 -to-village scale where incentives to invest in value-adding – a proces
 s traditionally centered in urban areas – are created (or eroded) in lo
 cations closer to the farm-forest edge. The paper thus seeks to build the
 ory about effective climate finance from the ground up\, drawing on lands
 capes where "actually existing REDD+" and recent rural electrification ar
 e overlapping in space and time\, but not yet sufficiently in practice.  
 Lunch will be provided.
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  This installment of the Tobias Center r
 esearch seminar series\, entitled "<span data-olk-copy-source="MessageBod
 y"><em>The grid's rural edge: Rethinking energy- and land-based climate f
 inance in upland Laos</em>\,"</span> features Mike Dwyer\, Associate Prof
 essor in IU's Department of Geography.</p><p><strong>Abstract:</strong><s
 pan data-olk-copy-source="MessageBody">Climate finance is often separated
  conceptually and operationally along lines that divide energy systems fr
 om land use. At a moment of urgency in both societal efforts to address c
 limate change and specialist debates about climate finance\, this paper p
 roblematizes the distinction between energy- and land-based climate finan
 ce by examining how rural electricity access influences land and forest d
 ynamics in the global South. Drawing on research in Laos\, the paper theo
 rizes what I call the land-energy nexus by examining the conditions under
  which rural electrification can help improve farmer-controlled value add
 ing\, and thus improve the performance of transnational efforts to reduce
  deforestation and forest degradation (REDD+). In doing so\, it seeks to 
 critically extend the concept of "land sparing"\, the theory of change th
 at underlies many REDD+ programs but has proven elusive in practice. The 
 paper proposes two intersecting scales for understanding the land-energy 
 nexus in the global South: first\, a system- or large-landscape scale whe
 re land-tenure security\, labor mobility and electricity financing each b
 ear on forest-change dynamics\; and second\, a more localized landscape-t
 o-village scale where incentives to invest in value-adding – a process 
 traditionally centered in urban areas – are created (or eroded) in loca
 tions closer to the farm-forest edge. The paper thus seeks to build theor
 y about effective climate finance from the ground up\, drawing on landsca
 pes where "actually existing REDD+" and recent rural electrification are 
 overlapping in space and time\, but not yet sufficiently in practice.</sp
 an></p><p>  Lunch will be provided.</p>
